<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ascoli Piceno</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Asculum, Asculum Picenum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ascoli Piceno is a city in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, on the Tronto river. As Asculum it was the chief town of the Picentes, and its massacre of Roman officials in 91 BCE started the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social War</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-pompeius-strabo">Gnaeus Pompeius Strabo</a> captured it in 89 BCE. Its historic centre, built largely of travertine, is known for the Piazza del Popolo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ascoli-piceno.jpg" alt="Ascoli Piceno" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: trolvag · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Centro_storico,_63100_Ascoli_Piceno_AP,_Italy_-_panoramio_(17).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ascoli Piceno lies at the confluence of the River Tronto and the small River Castellano and is surrounded on three sides by mountains. The Sibillini National Park borders it on the northwest and the Monti della Laga National Park on the south. National Road 4, the Salaria, links it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, following the Roman Via Salaria, the salt road that connected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latium">Latium</a> with the salt-producing areas of the Adriatic coast.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Asculum in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picenum">Picenum</a> was founded by the Italic Piceni and was originally a Sabine city. After its defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 268 BCE it became a civitas foederata with nominal independence. One account describes its people as warlike and bearing a generations-old grudge against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> for encroaching on their northern territories. Its siege in 89 BCE, after the Battle of Asculum, involved at least four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-legion">Roman legions</a> with Gallic and Spanish auxiliaries, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sling">sling</a> bullets found in the city show. It then became a municipium, and under the triumvirs or Augustus a colonia.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> of King Faroald ravaged it in 578. It was part of the Lombard Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a> from 593 to 789 and then ruled by vicars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> gained the most influence. A free republican commune was established in 1189, but internal strife opened the way to outside rulers such as the condottiero Galeotto I Malatesta and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-sforza">Francesco Sforza</a>, who was ousted in 1482. Ascoli then submitted again to papal suzerainty, and in 1860 it was annexed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Many buildings of the historic centre are of local travertine. Tradition holds that the city had some two hundred towers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and about fifty can still be seen. The Cathedral of Sant'Emidio houses an altarpiece by Carlo Crivelli. Roman remains include the Porta Gemina of the 1st century BCE, through which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-salaria">Via Salaria</a> entered the city, and the Ponte di Cecco over the Castellano. On the first Sunday in August a parade of more than 1,500 people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> costume honours Saint Emidio, protector of the city, and is followed by the Quintana, a tournament among knights of the six neighbourhoods.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ascoli Piceno” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pyrrhus of Epirus won costly victories at Heraclea in 280 BCE and <b>Asculum</b> in 279 BCE but withdrew after Beneventum in 275 BCE; in the First Punic War of 264–241 BCE Rome built fleets and forced Carthage out of Sicily after the Aegates Islands in 241 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When the tribune Marcus Livius Drusus, who proposed giving it to them, was murdered in 91 BCE, the Marsi, Paeligni, Samnites, Picentes and other peoples rose in revolt, starting with a massacre of Romans at <b>Asculum</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/italia-social-war-confederation">Italia (Social War confederation)</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Asculum</b> was their main centre, and they traded across the Adriatic and with Etruria.</blockquote><a href="/state/picentes">Picentes</a></li></ul></section>
